  "step_2": "The individual visualizes the probabilities 20% and 25% as 1/4 and 1/5, respectively, and imagines selecting one of these as 'balls' from a container. The individual notes that the 5% difference (between 20% and 25%) seems significant, while the 1000 Euro difference (4000 - 3000) does not feel large enough to justify choosing the lower probability. As a result, the individual decides for Lottery A. The deliberation focuses on the difference in probabilities (20% vs. 25%) and the perceived magnitude of the monetary difference (1000 Euros). The individual does not mention skewness, the distribution of outcomes, or the relative probability mass on zero outcomes. Instead, the reasoning centers on the perceived size of the probability difference and the monetary difference, suggesting a focus on risk preference or expected value differences rather than distributional skewness. The justification is based on intuitive perception of probability differences, not on the statistical property of skewness.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The report does not mention the distribution of outcomes, the probability mass on zero, or the asymmetry in the outcome structure. Instead, the individual focuses on the absolute difference in probabilities (5%) and the monetary difference (1000 Euros), which suggests a different reasoning process—possibly related to risk aversion or perceived magnitude of gains. The decision reason requires evaluating which lottery has more probability mass on unfavorable (zero) outcomes, which would make it more right-skewed. Lottery B has a higher probability of zero (80% vs. 75%), so it is more right-skewed. However, the individual does not reference this or any skewness-related comparison. The justification provided is entirely unrelated to skewness. Therefore, the decision reason was not used in the deliberation process.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

